Should I skip all whitespace or just spaces? Log lines may have leading spaces... The marker is immediately before. Use `trim()`-like semantics — skip any whitespace: `' \t\r\n'`. Actually `String.trim()` exists but operates on whole string. Let me skip whitespace chars via a small loop checking `line[start].trim().isEmpty`? That allocates per char. Better: check against a small set: ` == ' || == '\t' || == '\n' || == '\r'`. Keep it simple and explicit.

Hmm, actually simpler and cleaner: `final rest = line.substring(from).trimLeft();` hmm, that allocates a substring. Actually a cleaner idiom: 

```dart
var start = from;
while (start < line.length && _isWhitespace(line.codeUnitAt(start))) start++;
```

Or simply:

```dart
var start = from;
while (start < line.length && line[start].trim().isEmpty) {
  start++;
}
```

`line[start]` returns a single-char String; `.trim().isEmpty` is a clean idiom for whitespace check. Slightly allocating but fine for a log-line parser. Actually let me keep it dependency-free and simple with explicit chars. I'll use the trim() idiom — it's idiomatic Dart and readable.

